   "missing": "No single explicitly stated research-question sentence; inferred from objective framing."
  },
  "Q4": {
   "answer": "Four contributions: (1) a curated benchmark of 18 datasets across 9 task types with unified format/metrics; (2) a systematic zero-shot evaluation of 10 systems across 5 families showing in-domain performance does not predict OOD generalization; (3) analysis of annotation selection bias showing it understates non-lexical models; (4) an open-source Python package standardising loading, integration, and evaluation.",
   "locations": [
    "\u00a71 (contributions list)"
   ],
   "confidence": 5,
   "missing": ""
  },
  "Q5": {
   "answer": "BEIR is a heterogeneous zero-shot IR benchmark: 18 English datasets spanning 9 task families (bio-medical IR, QA, tweet, news, argument, duplicate-question, entity, citation, fact-checking), corpora from 3,633 to 14.9M documents, standardised on nDCG@10 via the TREC eval tool, released as a pip-installable package with unified corpus/query/qrels format.",
   "locations": [
    "\u00a73.1-3.5",
    "Abstract"
   ],
   "confidence": 5,
   "missing": ""
  },
  "Q6": {
   "answer": "Ten systems from five families (Lexical: BM25; Sparse: DeepCT, SPARTA, DocT5query; Dense: DPR, ANCE, TAS-B, GenQ; Late-interaction: ColBERT; Re-ranking: BM25+MiniLM cross-encoder) are evaluated from public pre-trained checkpoints with no target-domain fine-tuning; most trained on MS MARCO (DPR on four QA sets); documents truncated to 512 word-pieces; MS MARCO in-domain reported separately.",
